WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.249 --> 00:00:04.040
MARK HARNETT: It's a
fundamental assumption

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.110
that all mammalian
species are the same,

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:09.320
but it's really difficult to
actually show that that's true.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.560
So this was one of my
holy grail experiments,

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:15.480
was to look at the
biophysics of neurons,

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:18.390
how different inputs are
processed as they arrive

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:21.290
into neurons and integrate
it together and see

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:24.830
if that's different in
rodents and in humans.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:29.630
The challenge is getting the
tissue from neurosurgeons.

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:32.270
And those neurosurgeons have
to be research-oriented.

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:34.210
They have to want
to collaborate.

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:38.455
And we did that with
Syd Cash over at MGH.

00:00:38.455 --> 00:00:40.580
SYDNEY CASH: We obtained
brain tissue from patients

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:43.520
with intractable epilepsy who
are undergoing neurosurgery

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.060
to remove abnormal regions
deep within the brain.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.727
To do that, we had to remove
overlying cortex, that area

00:00:49.727 --> 00:00:52.310
of the brain that's thought to
be responsible for higher order

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:53.744
cognitive processing.

00:00:53.744 --> 00:00:55.160
We then took that
piece of tissue,

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:58.400
placed it into specialized
artificial cerebrospinal fluid,

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.257
and handed it to Mark's crew.

00:01:00.257 --> 00:01:02.090
MARK HARNETT: It's
usually Lou and Derrick--

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:05.090
basically are running down
the halls of MGH hospital

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:07.460
and out the door and
into a waiting car.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:09.260
And then they jump
out of the door

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:12.260
and then run it up to the
lab where we're waiting.

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:15.350
We take it out, put
it into a new solution

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:17.570
that's oxygenated and cold.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:22.970
And then we have to take this
out and orient it and figure

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:27.890
out, how are we going to set
it up on the slicer physically

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:32.560
so that we can get these
thin sheets of the circuit

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:34.670
to then go on the
microscope that we can then

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:36.110
use to do experiments?

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:42.102
Any misstep at this stage
basically ends the entire case.

00:01:42.102 --> 00:01:44.560
LOU BEAULIEU-LAROCHE: So I just
put one of the brain slices

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:45.740
on our microscope over here.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:47.365
MARK HARNETT: And
under the microscope,

00:01:47.365 --> 00:01:49.910
there's this moment in
which we bring the brain

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:55.460
slice into focus, and sometimes
there's just nothing there.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:56.520
It's just dead.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:57.940
But other times--

00:01:57.940 --> 00:01:58.980
LOU BEAULIEU-LAROCHE:
Here's a neuron there.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:00.021
There's another one here.

00:02:00.021 --> 00:02:03.110
MARK HARNETT: It's just filled
with live, fat, happy, juicy

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:04.100
looking neurons.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:05.975
LOU BEAULIEU-LAROCHE:
So I'm going to be here

00:02:05.975 --> 00:02:07.080
for 20 hours or something.

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:08.621
MARK HARNETT:
Everyone's very excited

00:02:08.621 --> 00:02:12.050
because it means
two days of data.

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.380
So the technique that we use to
record the electrical activity

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:18.410
from these neurons is
called patch clamping.

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:23.240
The key principle is you
take a piece of glass tubing,

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:25.910
and you pull it into a
little tiny hollow needle,

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:28.340
and then you fill it with
an electrolyte solution.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.770
So this glass needle
becomes an electrode.

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:32.311
MATHIEU LAFOURCADE:
And now I'm going

00:02:32.311 --> 00:02:34.130
to fill the pipette
with some dye.

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:37.010
MARK HARNETT: And the glass has
a really fascinating property,

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:40.105
in that it adheres very, very
tightly to the cell membrane.

00:02:40.105 --> 00:02:41.480
But this is one
of the challenges

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:42.710
to doing these
experiments-- is you've got

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:43.910
to make one of these pipettes.

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:45.320
You got to fill it with
electrolyte solution.

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:46.571
You got to put it on the ring.

00:02:46.571 --> 00:02:48.195
You've got to find
a neuron that you're

00:02:48.195 --> 00:02:50.310
interested in, that looks
good, that's healthy.

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.580
Then you got to get your
electrode down in there

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:54.690
into the solution
next to the neuron.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:56.570
It's like playing a
neuroscience video game.

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:58.070
MATHIEU LAFOURCADE:
So now I'm going

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:02.130
to put some positive
pressure because this

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:03.957
is connected to the pipettes.

00:03:03.957 --> 00:03:06.290
MARK HARNETT: You then push
the pipette into the neuron,

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:07.976
and then you release
the pressure.

00:03:07.976 --> 00:03:09.350
And this little
piece of membrane

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:11.720
now gets sucked up
into the pipette,

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:14.630
and then you can gain
intracellular access

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:16.920
to the inside of that cell.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.610
You can also use it to
fill the neuron with dye

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:23.180
so you can reveal the
architecture of those neurons.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:26.120
And then you can correlate the
architecture of the structure

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:27.290
with the function.

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:30.590
And so what Lou was trying to
do is have a recording electrode

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:34.040
near the soma but then
another electrode far away,

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:37.010
out in the antenna
structure, the dendrites,

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:40.430
to see what kinds of local
processing operations

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:42.140
are taking place out
there and then how

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:44.000
that dendrite communicates
with the soma.

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:50.240
And so we started
to do experiments

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.160
by just looking at
the dendrites that

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:53.990
are the closest to the soma.

00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:57.164
In that space, things
look very, very similar.

00:03:57.164 --> 00:03:59.330
So on one hand, that was a
little bit disappointing.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:01.760
We were expecting
specializations

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:03.530
that made us human.

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:06.030
But on the other hand,
it's reassuring because--

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:07.940
OK, rodents are a
really good model.

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:11.990
The past 50 years of doing
experiments in rodents

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:12.870
makes sense.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.420
They're an excellent
model system.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:16.970
Where we started
to see differences

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:20.750
was when we looked into the
very far away dendrites,

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:22.760
the ones that are much,
much longer than what

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:24.230
you see in that rodents.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:27.680
And there, we see that their
behavior is very different.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.670
Those very distal dendrites are
integrating a bunch of inputs

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:34.040
independently of what's
happening at the soma.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:37.190
So the human neurons form
their own individual kinds

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:38.060
of neural networks.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:40.370
It's a network inside a neuron.

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:43.400
So that conditional
kinds of interaction

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.671
supports a very particular
mode of computation

00:04:45.671 --> 00:04:47.420
in the human neurons,
so that's what we're

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:48.680
starting to see evidence for.

00:04:56.964 --> 00:04:58.630
MATHIEU LAFOURCADE:
Red Bull and coffee.

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:05.840
And-- no, no, no--

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:08.740
passion for science, of course.

